    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Lunda Empire was a tributary state of the Central African savanna, ruled by a sacred king titled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwaant-yaav">Mwaant Yaav</a>.</li><li>Positional succession and perpetual kinship let it absorb chiefdoms without removing their rulers, linking the interior to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>.</li><li>Succession disputes, the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chokwe">Chokwe</a> and colonial partition in the 1890s broke it up, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwaant-yaav">Mwaant Yaav</a> title survives.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Under perpetual kinship, a new Lunda office-holder inherited his predecessor's identity and relationships, so ties between chiefdoms could last for generations unchanged.</p></aside>
    </section>

